<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:progression="using:CcpClient.Desktop.Features.Progression"
             x:Class="CcpClient.Desktop.Views.Pages.IntakePage">
  <!--
    The Graded Intake page (WPF Views/Tabs/GradedIntakeTabView.xaml). Hop 2 of the route the
    rail door opens: the door NAVIGATES here (WPF's BtnNavGradedIntake -> ShowTab("gradedintake"),
    MainWindow/MainWindow.TabNavigation.cs:947) and the ONE window-opening button lives on this
    page — WPF's one-entry rule (MainWindow/MainWindow.Presets.cs:1007,1036).

    THE WORDS ARE WPF'S OWN. The title is the literal at GradedIntakeTabView.xaml:60 (deliberately
    not localized upstream: "the intake's own page content is English-only, and the launch button
    beside it has always been a literal too"), the sub-line is :67-68 verbatim, the section head is
    :125, its blurb is :127, and the button is :152's content emoji-stripped (the §9 D8 rule —
    the app strips emoji mod-aware, MainWindow/MainWindow.UiUpdates.cs:101,124) carrying :155's
    tooltip verbatim.

    THE WEEKLY-PASS GATE IS REAL AND IT REFUSES (§11 D26). Unlimited retakes are the patron
    privilege upstream — Premium is "unlimited runs, no week, no door"
    (Services/Progression/IntakePassService.cs:13) and "retakes stay a reason to subscribe" (:26-29)
    — so an ungated button would hand every user paid content. The band below carries the refusal.
    Two divergences from WPF's gate, both deliberate:
      * WPF PAINTS its gate at navigation, from a pass state it already knows
        (MainWindow.Lab.cs:368-434). The port's context — and therefore its pass — is prepared on
        the first press, so the band appears WHEN A PRESS IS REFUSED, exactly as the DTRH band does
        for the same class of reason (§10 D16).
      * WPF's gate is a CURTAIN: IsHitTestVisible="True" with GradedIntakeGatedContent.IsEnabled
        = false behind it (GradedIntakeTabView.xaml:191-192). The port's band is hit-test
        TRANSPARENT, because a band that appears after the first press and then swallowed the
        second would trap the user on a refusal that can expire at Monday 00:00 (§11 D34).

    WHAT IS OMITTED, AND WHY (§11 D27, D29). WPF's page also carries a pass banner (:104-124), the
    hidden classic-quiz controls (:131-147), a past-runs list (:166-175), a BETA pill (:62-64) and a
    gate CTA that opens App Info & Data. None is ported: no AI-availability source, no persisted
    past-run index, and nowhere for an unlock CTA to go (the §10 D17 rule). Rendering any of them
    would be the greyed control that swallows the gesture — the §9 D7 rule. ABSENT, not disabled.
  -->
  <!-- The page scrolls now that it carries a second module, the same way the System page does.
       PageHost is a bare ContentControl (MainWindow.axaml:486), so a page that outgrows the window
       is CLIPPED rather than scrolled, and the card is the part that would have gone off the bottom. -->
  <ScrollViewer>
  <StackPanel Margin="20" Spacing="4">
    <TextBlock Classes="page-title" Text="Graded Intake" />
    <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
               Text="A banded descent that reads how you answer - and how long you take - and drafts a personalised session from it." />

    <Border Classes="module" Margin="0,16,0,0">
      <Panel>
        <StackPanel Spacing="10">
          <TextBlock Classes="module-title" Text="Begin a run" />
          <TextBlock Classes="page-blurb" MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                     Text="Each intake runs in its own window. Answer honestly - the pacing of your answers counts as much as the answers themselves." />

          <!-- The one working destination on this page. GradedIntakeTabView.xaml:152 content is
               "✨ Begin Intake"; the emoji-stripped form is what the running app renders. NEVER
               disabled, in any branch: WPF's launch button is covered by the gate overlay, never
               greyed, so a refused press must ARRIVE and be answered out loud. -->
          <Button x:Name="BeginIntakeButton"
                  Classes="module-action"
                  AutomationProperties.AutomationId="BeginIntakeButton"
                  AutomationProperties.Name="Begin Intake"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                  ToolTip.Tip="A banded descent that drafts a personalised session from how you answer (Beta)."
                  Content="Begin Intake" />
        </StackPanel>

        <!-- The pass gate. WPF's scrim colour and radius (GradedIntakeTabView.xaml:192), the
             port's plate (the §10 D23 lesson: prose on a translucent scrim composites through the
             card underneath and cannot be read). IsHitTestVisible is on the ELEMENT, not in the
             style, because it is the load-bearing attribute of the whole surface. -->
        <Border x:Name="PassGate"
                Classes="intake-gate"
                IsHitTestVisible="False"
                IsVisible="False">
          <Border x:Name="PassGatePlate"
                  Classes="lock-plate"
                  AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PassGatePlate"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  Margin="16"
                  MaxWidth="620">
            <StackPanel Spacing="8">
              <TextBlock x:Name="PassGateTitle"
                         Classes="lock-title"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PassGateTitle"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="PassGateText"
                         Classes="lock-text"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="PassGateText"
                         TextAlignment="Left"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>
        </Border>

        <!-- THE FAILURE SURFACE. WPF's is a blocking MessageBox titled "Graded Intake"
             reading "Couldn't start Graded Intake:" plus the exception message, with
             MessageBoxImage.Warning (MainWindow/MainWindow.Lab.cs:161-166). The port had none: the
             button called IntakeLaunch.Launch() synchronously, so a throw while preparing the
             session context escaped the click handler into Avalonia's dispatcher with nothing on
             screen to show for it.

             The same reasoning as the Play page's fault band, and the same livery — one failure
             idiom for the port, two headlines, because WPF's two catches carry two different
             strings. It must not look like the pass gate above it: that gate can say "you already
             had your run this week", and a fault that wore those clothes would tell a user their
             week was gone when in fact the app broke. Divergence §13 D41. -->
        <Border x:Name="FaultBand"
                Classes="fault-band"
                IsHitTestVisible="False"
                IsVisible="False">
          <Border x:Name="FaultBandPlate"
                  Classes="fault-plate"
                  AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FaultBandPlate"
                  HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                  Margin="16"
                  MaxWidth="620">
            <StackPanel Spacing="8">
              <TextBlock x:Name="FaultBandTitle"
                         Classes="fault-title"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FaultBandTitle"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                         Text="" />
              <TextBlock x:Name="FaultBandText"
                         Classes="fault-text"
                         AutomationProperties.AutomationId="FaultBandText"
                         TextAlignment="Left"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap"
                         Text="" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Border>
        </Border>
      </Panel>
    </Border>

    <!--
      THE TRAINER CARD. Upstream's is a portrait with a wardrobe over it, a level, a rank, four
      pinned badges and a scene banner — eight MainWindow.Profile* partials, 3060 lines
      (client/docs/trainer-card-census.md §1.7). This port has exactly one piece of that: the
      graded-run award record the intake writes (Features/Progression/GradedRunAwards.cs), so the
      card renders THAT and says the rest out loud instead of showing it empty.

      It lives on this page rather than behind a rail door of its own, and that is the port's own
      rule rather than a shortcut: ShellRoutes.cs declares five doors and refuses a sixth whose
      destination is nearly empty ("a door that navigates to an empty room is the same
      unreachability the shell exists to end"). One module of real state does not fill a room. It
      sits on the page whose runs earn the awards, which is also where a user looks for them.

      WHAT IS NOT HERE, and is not here as a control either: no share, export, upload or publish
      button. Upstream's counterpart is the eleven-toggle leaderboard privacy dialog
      (Views/Controls/ProfilePrivacyPanel.xaml.cs:43-74) over real outbound traffic
      (Services/Progression/LeaderboardService.cs:16,106,174) — owner-gated and unapproved (census
      §7). A disabled share button would be the greyed control that swallows the gesture (§9 D7), so
      there is none to disable. No portrait, no wardrobe stage, no banner, no pinned badges: all of
      those need bytes and a compositor this build does not have (census §6.2 B3/B5).
    -->
    <Border Classes="module" Margin="0,16,0,0">
      <StackPanel Spacing="8">
        <TextBlock x:Name="TrainerCardTitle"
                   Classes="module-title"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="TrainerCardTitle" />

        <!-- The two absences a user would otherwise read as a broken card. Said in words, never
             rendered as an empty stat or a zero: no XP, level, streak or rank exists anywhere in
             client/src, so a "Level 0" here would be a number nothing in the app computes. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="TrainerCardLevelNote"
                   Classes="page-blurb"
                   MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="TrainerCardLevelNote" />
        <TextBlock x:Name="TrainerCardPortraitNote"
                   Classes="page-blurb"
                   MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="TrainerCardPortraitNote" />

        <!-- Only visible when the record has something to say for itself: never read, or read and
             failed. A card that could not read the record must not look like a card with nothing
             on it. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="TrainerCardRecordNote"
                   Classes="page-blurb"
                   Opacity="1"
                   MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="TrainerCardRecordNote"
                   IsVisible="False" />

        <ItemsControl x:Name="TrainerCardAwards"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="TrainerCardAwards">
          <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate x:DataType="progression:TrainerCardAward">
              <StackPanel Spacing="1" Margin="0,6,0,0">
                <!-- Name and requirement are upstream's own authored strings
                     (Models/Achievement.cs:663-701); the status line is what THIS build can stand
                     behind about them today. -->
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Name}" Classes="module-title" FontSize="13" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Requirement}" Classes="diagnostic" TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Status}" Classes="page-blurb" Opacity="0.9"
                           MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left" />
              </StackPanel>
            </DataTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
        </ItemsControl>

        <!-- The entitlement sentence (divergence D228) and the locality sentence. The first is why
             a patron-exclusive award appears here at all; the second is the whole of what this
             build does with it. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="TrainerCardTierNote"
                   Classes="diagnostic"
                   MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Margin="0,10,0,0"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="TrainerCardTierNote" />
        <TextBlock x:Name="TrainerCardLocalOnlyNote"
                   Classes="diagnostic"
                   MaxWidth="640" HorizontalAlignment="Left" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="TrainerCardLocalOnlyNote" />
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>
  </StackPanel>
  </ScrollViewer>
</UserControl>
